      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We need to solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\). To do this, we will consider the definition of the absolute value function and break it into cases based on the expression inside the absolute value.\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\). So the equation becomes:\n\\[ n + 6 = 2 - n. \\]\nWe can solve for \\( n \\) by adding \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ n + n + 6 = 2, \\]\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2. \\]\nNext, subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[ 2n = 2 - 6, \\]\n\\[ 2n = -4. \\]\nThen, divide both sides by 2:\n\\[ n = -2. \\]\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\). Substituting \\( n = -2 \\) into \\( n + 6 \\):\n\\[ -2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0. \\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution.\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) = -n - 6 \\). So the equation becomes:\n\\[ -n - 6 = 2 - n. \\]\nWe can solve for \\( n \\) by adding \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ -n + n - 6 = 2, \\]\n\\[ -6 = 2. \\]\nThis is a contradiction, so there is no solution in this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
