      "text": "Starting with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n|n + 6| = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nWe need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The equation $|x| = y$ implies that $x = y$ or $x = -y$. Therefore, we can split the absolute value equation into two cases:\n\n1. $n + 6 = 2 - n$\n2. $n + 6 = -(2 - n)$\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n**Case 1:**\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nTo isolate $n$, we add $n$ to both sides:\n\\[\nn + n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\nNext, subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = 2 - 6\n\\]\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\n\nFinally, divide by 2:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\n\n**Case 2:**\n\\[\nn + 6 = -(2 - n)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\nn + 6 = -2 + n\n\\]\n\nSubtract $n$ from both sides:\n\\[\n6 = -2\n\\]\n\nThis equation is a contradiction, meaning there is no value of $n$ that satisfies this case.\n\nThus, the only solution is from Case 1:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
